<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rába</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Raab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rába, in German Raab, is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> that rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and flows north-east through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to join a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at Győr. In 791 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> advanced as far as the Rába, and in 1664 the Battle of Saint Gotthard against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> was fought on its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raba.jpg" alt="Rába" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: hu:User:Fgg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raba-molnaszecsod-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
